{
  "dom_id": "step-q119840",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,800y-2{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=30\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14157279/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y(900-y),\\,y(0)=30.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=900.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $900.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
